<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as King Minos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minos is a legendary king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in Greek mythology, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and Europa, said to have ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. Greek writers remembered him as a lawgiver and as the first ruler to command the sea. The myths tell how he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a> build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a> and demanded a tribute of young Athenians, and after his death he became a judge of the dead. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation</a> after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minos.jpg" alt="Minos" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syriskos_Painter_-_ARV_259_1_-_Theseus_killing_the_Minotauros_in_the_presence_of_Minos_and_Ariadne_and_lookers_-_Athens_NAM_15115.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek tradition Minos and his brothers Rhadamanthys and Sarpedon were sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> and Europa, adopted and raised by King Asterion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. When Asterion died, Minos claimed the throne, and according to some sources he banished his brothers. To justify his rule he prayed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> for a sign, and Poseidon sent a giant white bull from the sea. Minos was bound to sacrifice it but substituted another bull, and in rage Poseidon cursed his wife Pasiphaë with a mad passion for the animal. She bore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>, which Minos shut in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a> built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a>.</p><p>Later poets and mythographers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, reconciled the contradictions in his character by assuming two kings of that name: a good Minos I, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and a harsher grandson, Minos II, the Minos of the myths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>, Pasiphaë and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a>. There is no evidence that Minos was a real person.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> shows Minos conversing with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> every nine years, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>'s reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> he took his laws from the god. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> says he was the first known king to build a navy, which he used against piracy to secure his revenue, and that he reigned over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands three generations before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>.</p><p>His son Androgeus was killed after winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaic Games</a>, by the bull at Marathon in one account and by jealous rivals in another. Minos made war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the plague and hunger sent on the city ended when it agreed to send seven boys and seven girls to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> every nine years to be fed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>. The hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> killed the Minotaur with the help of Minos's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the tale of his death Minos pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a> from city to city with a riddle about stringing a spiral shell, and learned where he was when King Cocalus at Camicus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> had it solved. Cocalus's daughters and Daedalus then scalded Minos to death in a bath.</p><p>After death Minos became a judge of the dead alongside his brother Rhadamanthys and half-brother Aeacus, and held the deciding vote. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> sets him at the entrance to the second circle of Hell, where he coils his tail to indicate each soul's circle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation</a> after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek memory linked the city to its Bronze Age past: the Iliad names Knossos first among the Cretan cities led by Idomeneus, and the Odyssey calls it the great city where <b>Minos</b> ruled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus calls him the first Greek after the legendary <b>Minos</b> to aim at mastery of the sea; he raided islands and mainland towns, captured Rhenaea and dedicated it to Apollo of Delos, and defeated a Lesbian fleet sent to help Miletus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Arthur Evans, who excavated Knossos from 1900, popularised the name “Minoan” after the mythical <b>King Minos</b> and divided Cretan prehistory into Early, Middle and Late Minoan phases on the evidence of pottery styles.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
